      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terraform for Aiven\nThe Aiven provider lets Terraform\nmanage Aiven projects, services\nand the objects inside services.\nAiven maintains it under the\nsource address `aiven/aiven`\n([provider docs](https://registry.\nterraform.io/providers/aiven/aiven\n/latest/docs)). The provider calls\nthe Aiven API with an API token\n([provider configuration](https://\ngithub.com/aiven/terraform-provide\nr-aiven/blob/v4.66.0/internal/plug\nin/provider.go#L71-L83)). It does\nnot connect to Kafka as a client.\nThe [Kafka map](../../kafka/kafka.\nmd#aiven-aiven) explains what\nAiven runs, and the [Terraform\nmap](../terraform.md) explains\nTerraform itself.\n\nThe unit is one Aiven project and\nthe services in it. Aiven groups\neverything in three levels: an\norganization holds projects, and a\nproject holds services ([Projects\nand Services](../../kafka/kafka.md\n#projects-and-services-aiven-proje\ncts-services)). A service is one\nrunning product, such as a Kafka\ncluster or a Kafka Connect\ncluster. Inside a Kafka service\nlive the objects that a data\nengineer touches most: topics,\naccess rules, schemas and\nconnectors.\n\nEach object has its own resource,\nand each resource has one key\nidea:\n\n- A service is a machine with\ndata. Its name is its identity, so\na new name means a new, empty\nservice ([A Service Holds Data](#a\n-service-holds-data-a-service-hold\ns-data)).\n- The advanced settings of a\nservice only grow. Removing a\nsetting from the code leaves the\nlive value in place ([Service\nSettings Only Grow](#service-setti\nngs-only-grow-service-settings-onl\ny-grow)).\n- A topic is known by its name. A\nnew name means a new, empty topic\n([The Topic Name Is the Key](#the-\ntopic-name-is-the-key-the-topic-na\nme-is-the-key)).\n- A connector is a flat map of\nstrings that the provider hardly\nchecks ([A Connector Is a Map of S\ntrings](#a-connector-is-a-map-of-s\ntrings-a-connector-is-a-map-of-str\nings)).\n- A schema subject is a list of\nversions that only grows ([Schema\nSubjects Only Grow](#schema-subjec\nts-only-grow-schema-subjects-only-\ngrow)).\n- Aiven has two kinds of access\nrule, and both can apply to one\nuser ([Two ACL Models](#two-acl-mo\ndels-two-acl-models)).\n\nTwo more ideas explain surprising\nplans. The provider is built on\ntwo Terraform libraries, and each\nresource behaves like its library\n([Two Libraries in One Provider](#\ntwo-libraries-in-one-provider-two-\nlibraries-in-one-provider)). Aiven\nalso changes some things on its\nown, outside any plan ([Aiven\nChanges Things Too](#aiven-changes\n-things-too-aiven-changes-things-t\noo)).\n\nNow compare the Snowflake\nprovider. Both manage a service\nthat someone else runs.\nSnowflake's provider speaks SQL,\nand Aiven's provider speaks REST.\nIn both, the most dangerous line\nin a plan is a replacement of an\nobject that holds data.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental\nModels explains the ideas above.\nReading an Aiven Configuration\nsays what you will see and where\nto look first. Pitfalls lists the\nfailures that experienced users\nplan around. Good and Bad\nDecisions compares the main design\nchoices.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-ef126ead0b76",
      "originalText": "Terraform for Aiven\nThe Aiven provider lets Terraform manage Aiven projects, services and the objects inside services. Aiven maintains it under the source address `aiven/aiven` ([provider docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/aiven/aiven/latest/docs)). The provider calls the Aiven API with an API token ([provider configuration](https://github.com/aiven/terraform-provider-aiven/blob/v4.66.0/internal/plugin/provider.go#L71-L83)). It does not connect to Kafka as a client. The [Kafka map](../../kafka/kafka.md#aiven-aiven) explains what Aiven runs, and the [Terraform map](../terraform.md) explains Terraform itself.\n\nThe unit is one Aiven project and the services in it. Aiven groups everything in three levels: an organization holds projects, and a project holds services ([Projects and Services](../../kafka/kafka.md#projects-and-services-aiven-projects-services)). A service is one running product, such as a Kafka cluster or a Kafka Connect cluster. Inside a Kafka service live the objects that a data engineer touches most: topics, access rules, schemas and connectors.\n\nEach object has its own resource, and each resource has one key idea:\n\n- A service is a machine with data. Its name is its identity, so a new name means a new, empty service ([A Service Holds Data](#a-service-holds-data-a-service-holds-data)).\n- The advanced settings of a service only grow. Removing a setting from the code leaves the live value in place ([Service Settings Only Grow](#service-settings-only-grow-service-settings-only-grow)).\n- A topic is known by its name. A new name means a new, empty topic ([The Topic Name Is the Key](#the-topic-name-is-the-key-the-topic-name-is-the-key)).\n- A connector is a flat map of strings that the provider hardly checks ([A Connector Is a Map of Strings](#a-connector-is-a-map-of-strings-a-connector-is-a-map-of-strings)).\n- A schema subject is a list of versions that only grows ([Schema Subjects Only Grow](#schema-subjects-only-grow-schema-subjects-only-grow)).\n- Aiven has two kinds of access rule, and both can apply to one user ([Two ACL Models](#two-acl-models-two-acl-models)).\n\nTwo more ideas explain surprising plans. The provider is built on two Terraform libraries, and each resource behaves like its library ([Two Libraries in One Provider](#two-libraries-in-one-provider-two-libraries-in-one-provider)). Aiven also changes some things on its own, outside any plan ([Aiven Changes Things Too](#aiven-changes-things-too-aiven-changes-things-too)).\n\nNow compare the Snowflake provider. Both manage a service that someone else runs. Snowflake's provider speaks SQL, and Aiven's provider speaks REST. In both, the most dangerous line in a plan is a replacement of an object that holds data.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental Models explains the ideas above. Reading an Aiven Configuration says what you will see and where to look first. Pitfalls lists the failures that experienced users plan around. Good and Bad Decisions compares the main design choices.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-aiven"
      }
    },
