    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7661423462,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-2fe1bc9b4e86",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1288.8,
      "y": 1160.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 420.0,
      "seed": 189023,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Schema Subjects Only Grow\nAn `aiven_kafka_schema` stands for one subject\nin the Schema Registry, which Aiven runs with\nKarapace ([Karapace](../../kafka/kafka.md#kara\npace-karapace)). A subject is a list of schema\nversions. An edit to the schema in Terraform\nregisters a new version; it never rewrites an\nold one. The plan checks the new schema\nagainst the version in the state, and it fails\nwith \"schema is not compatible with previous\nversion\" when the check fails ([schema resourc\ne](https://github.com/aiven/terraform-provider\n-aiven/blob/v4.66.0/internal/sdkprovider/servi\nce/kafkaschema/kafka_schema.go#L444-L501)).\n\nThe compatibility rule comes from\n`compatibility_level` on the subject, or from\nthe registry-wide\n`aiven_kafka_schema_configuration`. Destroying\nthat resource sets the global level back to\nBACKWARD ([schema configuration](https://githu\nb.com/aiven/terraform-provider-aiven/blob/v4.6\n6.0/internal/sdkprovider/service/kafkaschema/k\nafka_schema_configuration.go#L129-L150)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-2fe1bc9b4e86",
      "originalText": "Schema Subjects Only Grow\nAn `aiven_kafka_schema` stands for one subject in the Schema Registry, which Aiven runs with Karapace ([Karapace](../../kafka/kafka.md#karapace-karapace)). A subject is a list of schema versions. An edit to the schema in Terraform registers a new version; it never rewrites an old one. The plan checks the new schema against the version in the state, and it fails with \"schema is not compatible with previous version\" when the check fails ([schema resource](https://github.com/aiven/terraform-provider-aiven/blob/v4.66.0/internal/sdkprovider/service/kafkaschema/kafka_schema.go#L444-L501)).\n\nThe compatibility rule comes from `compatibility_level` on the subject, or from the registry-wide `aiven_kafka_schema_configuration`. Destroying that resource sets the global level back to BACKWARD ([schema configuration](https://github.com/aiven/terraform-provider-aiven/blob/v4.66.0/internal/sdkprovider/service/kafkaschema/kafka_schema_configuration.go#L129-L150)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "schema-subjects-only-grow"
      }
    },
