    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3165662983,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-5a33a3177131",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1321.2,
      "y": 2460.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 595.0,
      "seed": 332751,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Where to Look First\nRead an Aiven configuration in this order:\n\n1. `required_providers` and the lock file. The\nversion decides which resources use which\nlibrary, and which IMPORTANT changelog notes\napply.\n2. The `provider \"aiven\"` block and the token\nbehind it. The token decides which projects\nTerraform can reach and whether it can read\nsecrets.\n3. The `aiven_kafka` and `aiven_kafka_connect`\nblocks. Find where `project` and\n`service_name` come from, because they are the\nidentity. Then read `plan`,\n`termination_protection`, `lifecycle` and\n`kafka_user_config`.\n4. The `for_each` keys of topics, ACLs and\nconnectors. A changed key without a `moved`\nblock plans a destroy and a create.\n5. The topics. Check `partitions`,\n`replication` and the `config` keys. Note\ntopics that other tools also create, such as\ndead-letter topics of connectors.\n6. The ACLs. Check how far each wildcard\nreaches, and which users exist outside\nTerraform.\n7. The schemas and the registry-wide\ncompatibility level.\n8. The connectors. Check the target service,\nsecrets in `config`, `depends_on` on ACLs, and\nplugin version pins.\n9. The integrations and endpoints, and the\noutputs that other code reads.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-5a33a3177131",
      "originalText": "Where to Look First\nRead an Aiven configuration in this order:\n\n1. `required_providers` and the lock file. The version decides which resources use which library, and which IMPORTANT changelog notes apply.\n2. The `provider \"aiven\"` block and the token behind it. The token decides which projects Terraform can reach and whether it can read secrets.\n3. The `aiven_kafka` and `aiven_kafka_connect` blocks. Find where `project` and `service_name` come from, because they are the identity. Then read `plan`, `termination_protection`, `lifecycle` and `kafka_user_config`.\n4. The `for_each` keys of topics, ACLs and connectors. A changed key without a `moved` block plans a destroy and a create.\n5. The topics. Check `partitions`, `replication` and the `config` keys. Note topics that other tools also create, such as dead-letter topics of connectors.\n6. The ACLs. Check how far each wildcard reaches, and which users exist outside Terraform.\n7. The schemas and the registry-wide compatibility level.\n8. The connectors. Check the target service, secrets in `config`, `depends_on` on ACLs, and plugin version pins.\n9. The integrations and endpoints, and the outputs that other code reads.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "aiven-where-to-look-first"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1461646068,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-2c9934afea16",
